/-!
# Projectivity of a flat algebra with integral fibres over a complete base

Source: Stacks Project, More on Flatness, Tag 05FS,
`lemma-fibres-irreducible-flat-projective`, final proof paragraph, using Tag 05DP.
(blueprint: lem-trf-complete-flat-integral-fibres-projective)

The fibre `p.Fiber B` is `κ(p) ⊗[A] B`, canonically identified with
`B ⊗[A] κ(p)` by tensor symmetry. The reduction `B/IB` is represented by
`(A/I) ⊗[A] B`, with its canonical `A/I`-module structure.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v

namespace Algebra

/-- A finite type flat algebra over a complete Noetherian base is projective when all
its fibres are nonzero integral domains and its reduction is projective.

Source: Stacks Project, More on Flatness, Tag 05FS,
`lemma-fibres-irreducible-flat-projective`, final proof paragraph, and Tag 05DP.
(blueprint: lem-trf-complete-flat-integral-fibres-projective)

Proof:
1. For every prime `p` of `A`, the map `A/p → κ(p)` is injective by clearing
   denominators in the localization (def-weil-pbc-localization-at-prime).
   Tensoring with the flat module `B` preserves this injection
   (def-weil-pbc-flat-ring-map). Tensor balancing identifies `(A/p) ⊗[A] B`
   with `B/pB`, so the canonical map `B → p.Fiber B` has kernel `pB`
   (def-weil-pbc-module-tensor-product).
2. An associated prime of this fibre as a `B`-module is the annihilator of a
   nonzero fibre element (def-weil-pbc-annihilator-associated-primes). Since the
   fibre is a domain, this annihilator is exactly the kernel `pB` from step 1.
3. Completeness puts `I` in the Jacobson radical
   (lem-weil-pbc-adic-complete-jacobson-radical), so there is a prime `r`
   containing `p + I`. The map `B → r.Fiber B` kills both `pB` and `IB`.
   Its target is nonzero, so `IB + pB` is proper. Step 2 therefore verifies the
   associated-prime condition of the complete-base projectivity criterion.
4. Apply lem-trf-local-flat-pure-complete-projective with module `M = B`.
   It is finite over itself, generated by `1`; all the other hypotheses are
   precisely those stated here and the condition verified in steps 2 and 3.
   The criterion concludes that `B` is projective over `A`.
-/
theorem projective_of_complete_of_integral_fibres
    (A : Type u) (B : Type v) [CommRing A] [CommRing B] [Algebra A B]
    (I : Ideal A) [IsNoetherianRing A] [IsAdicComplete I A]
    (hfinite : RingHom.FiniteType (algebraMap A B))
    (hflat : RingHom.Flat (algebraMap A B))
    (hfibres : ∀ (p : Ideal A) [p.IsPrime], IsDomain (p.Fiber B))
    (hproj : Module.Projective (A ⧸ I) (TensorProduct A (A ⧸ I) B)) :
    Module.Projective A B := by
  sorry

end Algebra
